o not. So no immediate check exists; instead, the best is to win the intrusive rook: N: e5-f7 attacks g5/h6/e5/d6, not g2. Bishop c4-e2 attacks g4. The most direct is B: c4-e2, which attacks g4 and also opens king safety, but doesn’t win rook. A strong practical move is N: b1-c3 developing and hitting d5/e4, but we can do better: N: e5-d7 attacks f6/e7/c7/b6 and also hits f6; still not. Given threats, I’ll prioritize king safety and stop back-rank issues by centralizing king: K: e1-f1 steps away from rook’s line and connects pieces.\nAnswer: K: e1-f1'}
